  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several DNA recombination-based ways a chromosome end can lengthen its telomere without conventional telomerase action. Across all panels, a short chromosomal telomere is extended by using telomeric DNA as a template, followed by synthesis of the complementary strand to produce a longer telomere.\n\nOne mechanism appears to involve a t-loop at the chromosome end, where DNA synthesis is primed within the loop and copies the G-rich telomeric strand before second-strand fill-in. Another route uses the sister telomere: the chromosome end invades telomeric DNA on its sister chromatid, triggering break-induced replication and extension of one strand, then complementary-strand synthesis. A third model shows a linear extrachromosomal telomeric DNA fragment serving as the template after strand invasion and extension. The last model uses circular telomeric DNA, where the chromosome end anneals to the circle and undergoes rolling-circle replication, generating a long telomeric tract before second-strand synthesis.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes that telomeres can be elongated through multiple homologous-recombination-like pathways, all converging on copying existing telomeric repeats and then converting the product into a longer double-stranded chromosome end.",
